n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=1+1=2\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0=0\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the sets are orthonormal.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186599234 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$\n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=