rm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and second equations, we get ${x}_{3}=-{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=0$, respectively. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+0+(-{x}_{4})+{x}_{4} &=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186599253 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and 