\\,\\text{d}z.$*" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186607605 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186607605 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186608164 1071 "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186608164 1071 "Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186608168 1071 "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186608168 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186608170 1071 "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 8{x}^{3}\\,{7}^{5{x}^{4}+8}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186608170 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int 8{x}^{3}\\,{7}^{5{x}^{4}+8}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186608178 1071 "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{6}^{x}}{2-{6}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186608178 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{6}^{x}}{2-{6}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186608184 1071 "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{n}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186608184 1071 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{n}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186608189 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+6q=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+6{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+5(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +10\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -10\\alpha +2\\beta &=\\frac{5}{2}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{25}{104},$ $\\beta =\\frac{5}{104}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{25}{104}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{104}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{25}{104}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{104}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{25}{104}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{104}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-2A{e}^{-2t}-3B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{25}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{52}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2A{e}^{-2t}-3B{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{25}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{52}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{25}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{52}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{25}{52}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{5}{52}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.49,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186608189 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q