ac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186613814 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{5}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186613814 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{5}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186614580 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186614580 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186614580 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function, but integral I is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)=x-{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)-(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-x-(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-x+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-(x-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-x)^{4}+1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186614580 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function, but integral I is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)=x-{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)-(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-x-(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-x+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-(x-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-x)^{4}+1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+1) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186614618 1071 "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{1-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry." 13194139539078 175