 series $0.3+0.6+1.2+2.4+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.6}{0.3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.8-0.28+0.098-0.034\\,3+⋯$ starts with $n=1$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-0.28)}{0.8} \\\\  & =-0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and III converge." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186615402 1074 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{32}-⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{4}{1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.3+0.6+1.2+2.4+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.6}{0.3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.8-0.28+0.098-0.034\\,3+⋯$ starts with $n=1$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-0.28)}{0.8} \\\\  & =-0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and III converge." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186615404 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n}\n$$ ?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186615404 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n}$ ?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186615421 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{3}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186615421 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{3}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186615430 1071 "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186615430 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186615430 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2,\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{4\\cdot 5\\pi }{6}+4n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{10\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{10\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186615430 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the 